@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+---
+name: machine-first-game-review
+description: Run evidence-backed machine review, bounded automatic repair, and independent re-verification for Game-Codex browser-game, game-world, adult review/observer, responsive UI, accessibility, route, persistence, privacy, regression, smoke, or release-readiness changes. Use when implementing or assessing gameplay, child-facing game UI, adult game tools, cross-game shared runtime, or a formal machine verdict.
+---
+
+# Machine-First Game Review
+
+Make routine game QA an agent responsibility. Produce reproducible browser and logic evidence, repair in-scope defects, and ask the user only for questions a machine cannot honestly answer.
+
+## Workflow
+
+1. Read `AGENTS.md`, the applicable initiative guardrails, the canonical route/catalog sources, and the affected tests. Preserve unrelated worktree changes.
+2. State the bounded product question, child value, learning value, changed paths, and explicit non-goals. Keep accepted content and real-child gates frozen.
+3. Before a visual change, capture an isolated `PRE_CHANGE_REFERENCE`. Label it reference-only; do not call it an accepted baseline.
+4. Generate route/state inventory from the real router and `gameCatalog`. An action adapter may be keyed by game ID, but its keys must exactly match the catalog.
+5. Run hard gates and collect named evidence. Keep same-origin requests separate from forbidden external requests.
+6. Run the three semantic rubrics independently against common raw evidence. Do not let a later reviewer read an earlier reviewer finding file; merge only afterward.
+7. For a finding, record it, apply the smallest authorized repair, rerun dependency-affected deterministic/browser/semantic gates plus shared safety gates, and repeat at most three times.
+8. Once Sev-1/2 and blockers are clear, run the final full regressions, catalog smoke, machine review, tests, build, and independent acceptance against one unchanged file tree.
+9. Establish reviewed visual and ARIA baselines only after hard, semantic, accessibility, responsive, and independent checks pass. Then rerun without snapshot updates.
+10. Emit exactly one verdict: `PASS_MACHINE`, `AUTO_REVISE`, or `ESCALATE_HUMAN`.
+
+## Hard gates
+
+Require evidence for:
+
+- compile, targeted tests, full tests, and build;
+- route precedence, state invariants, save validation, migration, and corrupt-save recovery;
+- no console error or page error;
+- `SAME_ORIGIN_ALLOWED` and `EXTERNAL_NETWORK_FORBIDDEN` with zero external analytics, telemetry, CDN, remote font, API, or tracking requests;
+- privacy/PII denylist and local-only minimum evidence;
+- adult document scroll/reflow, one vertical owner, wheel, real touch gesture, PageDown, End, last-control focus, and no horizontal overflow;
+- keyboard focus, visible focus, unobscured focus, target size, and click alternative for drag;
+- accessible headings, roles, names, labels, dialogs, and no duplicate IDs;
+- deterministic visual states with no overlap, clipping, corruption, or unexplained reference diff;
+- child-safe copy and absence of forbidden pressure mechanics.
+
+Use isolated Playwright contexts and synthetic `localStorage` for every fresh, repaired, returning, corrupt, or fixture state. Never launch the family Chrome profile or clear its persistent state.
+
+## Semantic rubrics
+
+Run separately:
+
+- `R1_CHILD_FIRST_UX`: game-versus-teacher-catalog feel, primary action, child hierarchy, cognitive load, age-appropriate copy, pressure/shame, and adult chrome leakage.
+- `R2_VISUAL_ACCESSIBILITY`: hierarchy, consistency, feedback readability, overlap, clipping, contrast, focus, targets, responsive layout, modal behavior, and reduced motion.
+- `R3_ADVERSARIAL_QA`: rapid/wrong input, refresh, back/forward, corrupt save, observer disconnect, mute, keyboard, touch, return loops, and route conflicts.
+
+Every reviewer output must state `reviewEngine`, `model`, `reviewMode`, and `evidenceFiles`. `reviewMode` must be exactly `INDEPENDENT_MODEL`, `INDEPENDENT_SUBAGENT`, or `RUBRIC_SEPARATED_SAME_MODEL`. Never fabricate model independence. Each finding must contain `id`, `reviewer`, `route`, `state`, `severity`, `category`, `visibleEvidence`, `whyItMatters`, `suggestedFix`, and `confidence`, and cite a real screenshot, ARIA snapshot, trace/event record, or route/state artifact.
+
+## Verdict and escalation
+
+`PASS_MACHINE` requires 100% hard-gate PASS, zero Sev-1/2 findings, every playthrough profile PASS, the adult scroll matrix PASS, no unresolved critical reviewer conflict, zero external-network or privacy failure, and final full tests/build PASS.
+
+Use `AUTO_REVISE` while an authorized repair remains within the three-loop limit.
+
+Use `ESCALATE_HUMAN` only for:
+
+- `REAL_CHILD_BEHAVIOR_REQUIRED`;
+- `VALUE_OR_PREFERENCE_CHOICE`;
+- `IRREVERSIBLE_PRIVACY_OR_PUBLICATION`;
+- `MACHINE_REVIEWER_CRITICAL_DISAGREEMENT`;
+- `THREE_REPAIR_LOOPS_FAILED`.
+
+Ask one concrete question with evidence, attempted repairs, two or three options, a recommendation, and uncertainty. Do not generate a routine parent acceptance workbench.
+
+## Limits
+
+Machine review can prove technical behavior and visible structure. It cannot prove child fun, long-term engagement, learning effectiveness, retention, family preference, or promotion. Synthetic tooling is never real-child evidence. Technical `PASS_MACHINE` never substitutes for a real child, parent, or teacher gate.
